"Time-Optimal Interactive Proofs for Circuit Evaluation (https://eprint.iacr.org/2013/351)\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2013/351\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 73\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of cite0†tin foil†iacr.org ?\nL2: \nL3: cite1†Cryptology ePrint Archive L4: \nL5:   * cite2†Papers Updates from the last:\nL6:     * cite3†7 days L7:     * cite4†31 days L8:     * cite5†6 months L9:     * cite6†365 days L10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * cite7†Listing by year L13:     * cite8†All papers L14:     * cite9†Compact view L15:     * cite10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org L16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * cite11†How to cite L19:     * * * *\nL20:     * cite12†Harvesting metadata L21:   * cite2†Submissions L22:     * cite13†Submit a paper L23:     * cite14†Revise or withdraw a paper L24:     * cite15†Acceptance and publishing conditions L25: \nL26: cite16†Image: Search Button L27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2013/351\nL30: \nL31: ### cite17†Time-Optimal Interactive Proofs for Circuit Evaluation L32: \nL33: Justin Thaler\nL34: ##### Abstract\nL35: Several research teams have recently been working toward the development of practical general-purpose protocols for verifiable computation. These protocols enable a computationally weak verifier to offload computations to a powerful but untrusted prover while providing the verifier with a guarantee that the prover performed the requested computations correctly. Despite substantial progress, existing implementations require further improvements before they become practical for most settings.\nL36: The main bottleneck is typically the extra effort required by the prover to return an answer with a guarantee of correctness, compared to returning an answer with no guarantee. We describe a refinement of a powerful interactive proof protocol due to Goldwasser, Kalai, and Rothblum. Cormode, Mitzenmacher, and Thaler show how to implement the prover in this protocol in time $O(S \\log S)$, where $S$ is the size of an arithmetic circuit computing the function of interest.\nL37: Our refinements apply to circuits with sufficiently ``regular'' wiring patterns; for these circuits, we bring the runtime of the prover down to $O(S)$. That is, our prover can evaluate the circuit with a guarantee of correctness, with only a constant-factor blowup in work compared to evaluating the circuit with no guarantee.\nL38: We argue that our refinements capture a large class of circuits, and we complement our theoretical results with experiments on problems such as matrix multiplication and determining the number of distinct elements in a data stream. Experimentally, our refinements yield a 200x speedup for the prover over the implementation of Cormode et al., and our prover is less than 10x slower than a C++ program that simply evaluates the circuit.\nL39: Along the way, we describe a special-purpose protocol for matrix multiplication that is of interest in its own right. Our final contribution is the design of an interactive proof protocol targeted at general data parallel computation. Compared to prior work, this protocol can more efficiently verify complicated computations as long as that computation is applied independently to many different pieces of data.\nL40: Note: This is the full version of a Crypto 2013 paper by the same title. This version corrects a typographical error in Section 7. We are grateful to Michael Walfish for identifying the error.\nL41: ##### Metadata\nL42: Available format(s)\nL43:     cite17†PDF L44: Category\nL45:     cite18†Foundations L46: Publication info\nL47:     Published elsewhere. This is the full version of a Crypto 2013 paper by the same title.\nL48: Keywords\nL49:     cite19†interactive proofs cite20†verifiable computation cite21†circuit evaluation L50: Contact author(s)\nL51:     justin thaler @ georgetown edu\nL52: History\nL53:     2017-02-08: last of 2 revisions\nL54:     2013-06-10: received\nL55:     cite22†See all versions L56: Short URL\nL57:     cite23†https://ia.cr/2013/351†ia.cr L58: License\nL59: \nL60: cite24†CC BY†creativecommons.org L61: BibTeX cite25†Image Copy to clipboard\nL62: \nL63:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2013/351,\nL64:           author = {Justin Thaler},\nL65:           title = {Time-Optimal Interactive Proofs for Circuit Evaluation},\nL66:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2013/351},\nL67:           year = {2013},\nL68:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2013/351}\nL69:     }\nL70: \nL71: \nL72: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJagged Polynomial Commitments (or: How to Stack Multilinears) (https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/917)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/917\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 81\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of cite0†tin foil†iacr.org ?\nL2: \nL3: cite1†Cryptology ePrint Archive L4: \nL5:   * cite2†Papers Updates from the last:\nL6:     * cite3†7 days L7:     * cite4†31 days L8:     * cite5†6 months L9:     * cite6†365 days L10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * cite7†Listing by year L13:     * cite8†All papers L14:     * cite9†Compact view L15:     * cite10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org L16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * cite11†How to cite L19:     * * * *\nL20:     * cite12†Harvesting metadata L21:   * cite2†Submissions L22:     * cite13†Submit a paper L23:     * cite14†Revise or withdraw a paper L24:     * cite15†Acceptance and publishing conditions L25: \nL26: cite16†Image: Search Button L27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2025/917\nL30: \nL31: ### cite17†Jagged Polynomial Commitments (or: How to Stack Multilinears) L32: \nL33: Tamir Hemo, Succinct\nL34: \nL35: Kevin Jue, Succinct\nL36: \nL37: Eugene Rabinovich, Succinct\nL38: \nL39: Gyumin Roh, Succinct\nL40: \nL41: Ron D. Rothblum, Succinct\nL42: ##### Abstract\nL43: Modern SNARK constructions, almost ubiquitously, rely on a polynomial commitment scheme (PCS) --- a method by which a prover can commit to a large polynomial $P$ and later provide evaluation proofs of the form \"P(x)=y\" to the verifier.\nL44: In the context of zkVMs (i.e., proof-systems for general-purpose RAM computations), the common design is to represent the computation trace as a sequence of tables, one per CPU instruction, and commit to the these tables, or even their individual columns, as separate polynomials. Committing separately to these polynomials has a large overhead in verification costs, especially in hash-based systems. In this work we drastically reduce this cost via a new construction which we call the jagged PCS.\nL45: This PCS enables the prover to commit to the entire computation trace as a single polynomial, but then allows for the verifier to emulate access to the individual table or column polynomials, so that the arithmetization can proceed in the usual manner. The jagged PCS may be thought of as a sparse PCS for a very particular form of sparsity - namely, a \"jagged\" matrix in which each column has a different height. Our construction of the jagged PCS is highly performant in practice.\nL46: In contrast to existing sparse PCS constructions for general sparse polynomials, the jagged PCS does not require the prover to commit to any additional oracles and the prover cost is dominated by 5 finite field multiplications per element in the trace area. Furthermore, we implement the verifier as an arithmetic circuit that depends only on the total trace area - thereby significantly reducing the problem of \"combinatorial explosion\" often encountered in zkVM recursion.\nL47: ##### Metadata\nL48: Available format(s)\nL49:     cite17†PDF L50: Category\nL51:     cite18†Cryptographic protocols L52: Publication info\nL53:     A minor revision of an IACR publication in EUROCRYPT 2026\nL54: Keywords\nL55:     cite19†PCS cite20†Sparse PCS cite21†Jagged PCS L56: Contact author(s)\nL57:     tamir @ succinct xyz\nL58: kevin @ succinct xyz\nL59: eugene @ succinct xyz\nL60: min @ succinct xyz\nL61: ron @ succinct xyz\nL62: History\nL63:     2026-04-13: revised\nL64:     2025-05-21: received\nL65:     cite22†See all versions L66: Short URL\nL67:     cite23†https://ia.cr/2025/917†ia.cr L68: License\nL69: \nL70: cite24†CC BY†creativecommons.org L71: BibTeX cite25†Image Copy to clipboard\nL72: \nL73:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2025/917,\nL74:           author = {Tamir Hemo and Kevin Jue and Eugene Rabinovich and Gyumin Roh and Ron D. Rothblum},\nL75:           title = {Jagged Polynomial Commitments (or: How to Stack Multilinears)},\nL76:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2025/917},\nL77:           year = {2025},\nL78:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/917}\nL79:     }\nL80: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/blob/main/LICENSE-MIT\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/blob/main/LICENSE-MIT: Cache miss\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view0\",\"id\":70}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/commi